course: everyday
lesson: 454
title: Forgiving
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: After Chloe spilled Mia's secret and lied about it, she came to Mia with a real sorry — not a mumbled «soz», but «I told your secret and then lied about it, and that was horrible. I'm sorry.» A real sorry names what happened. Mia said, «Okay. I forgive you. But I'm not telling you secrets for a while.» «Fair,» said Chloe. A month later they were friends again. Liam's teacher put it this way: «Forgiving isn't saying it was okay. It's saying you won't keep punishing them for it.»
explain: Forgiving doesn't cancel what's fair. Aisha forgave the friend who pretended she'd paid back £5 — and asked her to pay it back. She did. «Forgiving her didn't mean she keeps my fiver.» Ruby's friend said sorry for faking illness to miss her party. «I forgive you, but you have to come to the next one.» She came, with a cake. Sophie lied to her mum about where she'd been. Mum said, «I forgive you. But you'll text me every time for a month.» Sophie grumbled, but did it, and after a month Mum said «Thank you — I trust you again.» Rules after a lie aren't revenge; they're rebuilding.
explain: Late truth is still truth. Leo's big brother said Leo broke the telly, and Leo got grounded. A week later the brother couldn't stand it and confessed. Leo was raging («raging» means furious), then his brother gave him his Xbox time for a week and said sorry to Mum and Dad too. Leo forgave him. In Kai's class a girl lied about Kai to the teacher out of jealousy. When it came out, she cried and said sorry. Kai said, «It's okay. Just don't do it again.» By the end of the year they were friends. People who've done wrong can change; forgiving gives them the chance.
explain: Forgiving can take time, and you don't always have to feel it straight away. Jack's dad said, «I let you down and lied about it. I'm really sorry, son.» Jack said «It's okay», but it didn't feel okay yet — he felt better only after Dad came to the next three matches. Saying «I'm still upset, but I'm trying» is honest. Callum's mate in Glasgow kept telling lies — about his games, his dad, his football. Callum forgave him every time. Then one day: «Mate, I'll stay pals, but I'm no believing anything you say till you stop.» His mate went quiet, then slowly stopped.
explain: Sometimes there's no sorry at all. Theo's friend lied about him in a group chat and never apologised. Theo stopped being friends with him. Months later, Theo said hi in the corridor; he felt lighter, though they never became close again. Forgiving can mean letting go for yourself. And in Kirsty's family in Aberdeen the rule was: «Tell the truth straight away — half the trouble.» Her brother broke a lamp, told Mum at once, and only had to help clean up. Forgiveness comes easier when the truth comes fast. You can forgive and still remember the lesson; that isn't a grudge, it's being wise.
fact: Forgiving means choosing not to keep punishing someone; it does not mean saying what they did was okay.
fact: A real apology names what happened, for example «I told your secret and then lied about it».
fact: You can forgive someone and still be careful about trusting them for a while.
fact: Forgiving does not cancel what is fair; money borrowed should still be paid back.
fact: Rules or conditions after a lie, like texting home for a month, are for rebuilding trust, not for revenge.
fact: Owning up late is better than never owning up; making things right helps forgiveness.
fact: Forgiveness can take time; it is honest to say «I'm still upset, but I'm trying».
fact: You can forgive a friend who lies often and still tell them you won't believe them until they stop.
fact: Forgiving can mean letting go of anger for your own sake, even without a sorry.
fact: Telling the truth quickly usually means smaller trouble and easier forgiveness.
fact: People who have done wrong can change, and forgiveness gives them the chance.
fact: You can forgive and still remember what happened; remembering the lesson is not the same as holding a grudge.
fact: «Raging» (UK, Ireland) means furious; «pals» means friends; «no» in Scots can mean «not», as in «I'm no believing».
exercise: What made Chloe's sorry a real one? | She named exactly what she had done | She said «soz» very quickly and walked off | She bought Mia a present from the shop | She cried in front of everyone in the class
exercise: What did Mia say after forgiving Chloe? | No more secrets for a while | Let's forget it ever happened | You can tell all my secrets now | We can never be friends again
exercise: What did Liam's teacher say forgiving is? | Not punishing someone any more for it | Saying that what they did was okay really | Pretending it never happened at all | Getting your revenge first
exercise: Did forgiving her friend mean Aisha gave up her £5? | No, she asked for it back and got it | Yes, forgiving means losing the money too | Yes, but she got half of it back | No, she took £10 instead
exercise: What did Ruby ask from the friend who missed her party? | To come to the next one | To pay for the party | To say sorry to her mum | To never come again
exercise: Why did Sophie's mum ask her to text every time for a month? | To rebuild trust after the lie | To punish her as revenge for lying | To use up all her phone credit | Because Mum likes texts
exercise: What happened after Sophie kept to the rule? | Mum said «I trust you again» | Mum made it two months | Mum took her phone away | Nothing ever changed
exercise: What did Leo's brother do to make things right? | Confessed, said sorry and gave Leo his Xbox time | Broke another thing so they'd share the blame | Paid for a brand-new telly from his savings | Said nothing and hoped it would all blow over
exercise: Why had the girl lied about Kai to the teacher? | She was jealous | She was confused | She was bored in class | She was told to do it
exercise: What happened between Kai and that girl by the end of the year? | They became friends | They never spoke again | She moved schools | He told on her
exercise: Why did Jack say «it's okay» but not feel it? | Forgiving can take time | He was lying to his dad | He didn't care about football | His dad didn't say sorry
exercise: What did Callum tell his mate who kept lying? | I'll stay pals, but won't believe you till you stop | You're not my pal any more, ever, so go away now | Tell me more lies, they're funny and I like them | I'll lie to you back from now on
exercise: Theo never got a sorry. How did letting go help him? | He felt lighter | He got a prize for it | His friend paid him back | Everyone clapped him
exercise: What was the rule in Kirsty's family? | Tell the truth straight away, half the trouble | Whoever breaks something pays for it all twice | Always blame the youngest one in the house | Hide broken things in the garden
exercise: What happened when Kirsty's brother owned up about the lamp at once? | He only had to help clean up | He was grounded for a year | He had to buy three lamps | He lost his birthday
exercise: Is remembering what happened the same as holding a grudge? | No — remembering the lesson is being wise | Yes, remembering it is always holding a grudge | Yes, you have to forget it to forgive | No, you must forget everything
test: Friend: «soz». That's it. What would make it a real sorry? | Saying what she did and that it was wrong | Saying «soz» louder, so everyone hears it | Sending a sad emoji and a broken heart | Buying sweets without a word
test: Your mate says «if you forgive someone you have to trust them 100% straight away». Is that right? | No — you can forgive and trust slowly | Yes, or else it isn't real forgiveness | Yes, but that's only for best friends | No, you must never trust again
test: Leo, raging: «he LIED and I got grounded!» A week later brother confesses. What does «raging» mean? | Furious | Racing | Bored | Happy
test: Aisha: «I forgive you — and I'll have my fiver back, ta.» Is that fair? | Yes, forgiving doesn't cancel what's fair | No, forgiving means forgetting the money | No, she should ask for a whole tenner now | Yes, but only after a year
test: Glasgow lad: «I'm no believing a word till you stop, pal.» What does «I'm no believing» mean? | I'm not believing | I'm now believing | I'm nearly believing | I'm not breathing
test: Mum: «Text me every time you go out for a month.» Sophie: «That's revenge!» What's closer to the truth? | It's rebuilding trust | It's a punishment forever | It's a game Mum likes playing | It's because Mum is bored
test: Jack: «Dad said sorry but I'm still well upset.» What's honest to say to Dad? | I'm still upset, but I'm trying | It's fine, it never happened | I'll never forgive you | You're the worst dad ever
test: Kirsty's bro: «I broke the lamp, Mam, sorry.» Right away. What happens in their house? | Half the trouble — he helps clean up | Double the trouble for owning up to it | Nothing, because lamps are dead cheap | He has to move out
test: What would happen if Kai had never forgiven the girl who lied? | They might never have become friends | She would have lied about him again for sure | Kai would get a prize for being tough | The teacher would forgive her instead
test: In a cartoon, after a sorry everyone hugs and it's all instantly perfect. What's more common in real life? | Forgiveness and trust take some time | Everything is perfect at once | Nobody ever forgives anyone | Sorry makes people forget
test: Theo's ex-friend never says sorry. Theo says hi in the corridor months later. What has Theo done? | Let go of the anger for himself | Become best friends with him all over again | Told everyone the friend was right | Pretended it never happened
exam: Identify the principal meaning of forgiveness as defined by the teacher. | The decision to cease punishing a person for a wrong | The declaration that the wrong was in fact acceptable | The obligation to forget the wrong entirely | The restoration of complete trust forthwith
exam: It is given that a borrower who falsely claimed repayment has apologised and been forgiven. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The sum borrowed remains properly repayable | The debt is extinguished by the forgiveness | The lender must apologise in turn | The borrower is entitled to borrow further
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises conditions imposed following a falsehood, such as a requirement to report one's whereabouts? | They serve to rebuild trust rather than to exact revenge | They serve exclusively as revenge for the falsehood told | They are imposed permanently upon the offender | They indicate that forgiveness was refused
exam: Determine which of the following is consistent with the principle that forgiveness and remembrance may coexist. | One may forgive a person while retaining the lesson of the wrong | One must forget a wrong entirely in order to forgive the person | Remembrance of a wrong constitutes a grudge | Forgiveness requires the wrong to be repeated
